Modern data center workloads present significant challenges to the existing dynamic power management (DPM) framework in Linux. Existing DPM frameworks trade off Quality of Service (QoS) awareness for responsiveness, oftentimes leaving significant energy-saving opportunities on the table. In this work, we present eBPF Governors, a framework to deliver highly responsive, low-overhead, QoS-aware dynamic power management by leveraging the benefits of eBPF, an emerging Linux kernel technology. eBPF Governors achieves this goal by (1) moving the P-state decision algorithm into eBPF programs in kernel space, (2) providing QoS-awareness to Hardware Managed P-States (HWP) by guiding performance preference knobs, (3) providing low-overhead QoS reporting into kernel space by leveraging asynchronous eBPF Map mechanisms, and (4) bypassing high-overhead QoS feedback by intercepting network-based performance feedback through eXpress Data Path (XDP) facilities provided by eBPF. We demonstrate that under high load volatility, eBPF Governors achieves 2-6x more power savings compared to existing state-of-the-art governors and 5.3% workload latency reduction by avoiding contention due to userspace syscall updates.